            "privateNotes": "",
            "publicNotes": "<p>Some say that when you gaze into the void it gazes back. But what of those who channel it?</p>\n<p>Assassins for hire tend to be cold pragmatists who might do anything for the right price, but many have their own strange philosophies or belong to obscure cults and religious sects. Deities popular with professional killers include the nihilistic Devourer and cunning Lao Shu Po. Some solarians, like the one below, justify their assassinations as a part of the Cycle necessary to balance the Universe.</p><h2>Mercenaries</h2><p>So long as the almighty credit can buy power, safety, and comfort, it can also be used to buy violence from the desperate, irrational, and unsentimental. Absalom Station is home to countless mercenary companies and freelance soldiers-for-hire, all eagerly offering their services to the highest bidder.</p>\n<p>Some mercs have a moral code or sense of honor that limits what contracts they accept, and Absalom law strictly limits what jobs a paramilitary company can hand out to its members. Of course, this doesn't stop a festering underbelly of wetwork and worse from blooming in the darker reaches of the station, if one knows where to look.</p>\n<p>Mercenaries aren't always operatives or soldiers. Sometimes they're cunning spellcasters, powerful solarians, or treacherous envoys. Bounty hunters are one of the most common class of merc hanging around the station. The bounty hunter below is an example of an elite foe tracking down dangerous outlaws, but the statistics for gangsters can stand in for lower-level mercenaries.</p>",
            "publication": {
                "license": "ORC",
                "remaster": true,
                "title": "Starfinder Absalom Station"
            }
        },
